592186045417] [17592186134933 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e6742f0e-ef7b-5dd1-89f2-b67bd6968d33" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134933 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134934 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134934 #edb/ref 1061 44755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134934 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ce3f9be3-b095-5f66-a582-c68d7cb49d78" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134934 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134935 #edb/ref 1061 44758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134935 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d66524f2-18df-53a2-9007-6aaa99c8c6f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134935 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134936 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134936 #edb/ref 1061 44794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134936 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f13db816-3a2c-5bc5-86bb-261c0c695783" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134936 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134939 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134939 #edb/ref 1061 45010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134939 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "91aeb514-1bab-5aae-a601-fdf72405bbbf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134939 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135160 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider a two-dimensional vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ on $\\mathbb R^2$ with component functions $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ defined over a region $D.$ \n\nRecall that Green's theorem in flux form states that the sum of the microscopic \"fluxes\" (divergences) of $\\mathbf{F}$ inside $D$ equals the macroscopic flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across the boundary of $D.$ \n\nWe can express Green's theorem in flux form as a single picture, shown below.\n\n![](images/52/52191ef5fea6e08b885dfd9ffea547e3843b8113847b532f8b4aa5d966d15213.png)\n\n    \n\nWe'll now state the higher-dimensional analog of Green's theorem in flux form. Please note that we will restrict our attention to so-called **simple regions** for the remainder of this lesson. Simple regions in $\\mathbb R^3$ can be classified as type I, type II, *and* type III." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135160 #edb/ref 1065 7606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135160 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6855af2b-f345-5f4d-aae2-49d6ee758d9d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135160 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135164 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the vector field \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = \\langle 2x+1,\\: 3z,\\: xy \\rangle\n$$\n\nand the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown below. \n\n![](images/cf/cf90197ac99a066db75154200de4cbee2494fef15deed6be7b7c4cd501ae8f26.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S.$\n\nTo find the total flux directly, we would need to find the flux through each of the six surfaces of our rectangular solid and then add the results. However, using the divergence theorem\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n= \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V,\n$$\n\nwe can reduce this computation to a single triple integral.\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+1)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy) \\\\  & =2+0+0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux through $S$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}2\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =2{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135164 #edb/ref 1065 7732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135164 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e318cc2d-81e3-5cbd-b0ce-2f076bfd06b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135164 #edb/ref 1067 "Calculating the Flux Across a Surface" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135170 #edb/ref 1064 "The **divergence theorem** is a higher-dimensional analog of Green's theorem in flux form. It states the following:\n\n*Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then*\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V\n=\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathbf{n}\\:\\mathrm{d}S\n$$\n\n*where $\\mathbf{n}$ is the outward-pointing unit normal to $S.$*\n\nIn other words, the sum of the microscopic \"fluxes\" (divergences) of $\\mathbf{F}$ inside $R$ equals the macroscopic flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through the boundary of $R.$ \n\nThe divergence theorem can be summarized in a single diagram.\n\n![](images/ff/ffabcceb5f4fd520a57f909c476b267f584ac4dc3fb948a5c1996b71dbc74e6f.png)\n\nThe divergence theorem can be used to simplify flux calculations. Calculating the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across a surface can be time-consuming because we often need to consider the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across multiple faces that make up $S.$ The divergence theorem often allows us to reduce a complicated flux calculation to a single triple integral." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135170 #edb/ref 1065 7773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135170 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ca1c4682-71c7-5950-950a-a33ad1e84c23" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135170 #edb/ref 1067 "The Divergence Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430911 #edb/ref 1080 "2x+xz" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430911 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430911 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "069804e3-7c58-5f7e-b7b1-a0c2c5c704f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430912 #edb/ref 1080 "2+z" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430912 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430912 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "06c35222-68