x=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=1,$ and the surfaces $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=x-z+2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=z\\,i-4y\\,j+{z}^{3}\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430947 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "00083281-29d7-555a-802b-bb136fd26b95" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430947 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-4y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{3}) \\\\  & =0-4+3{z}^{2} \\\\  & =3{z}^{2}-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}(3{z}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type III solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x-z+2\\}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(3{z}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x-z+2}\\,3{z}^{2}-4\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430948 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186430946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430948 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162595" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430948 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430948 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ $z=-1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=y{z}^{2}\\,i-2yz\\,j+3{z}^{2}\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430948 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "09c354f4-ffe0-5d80-b23d-97b7671b8b06" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430948 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2yz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =0+(-2z)+6z \\\\  & =4z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}4z\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}4z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{2}4z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =(2-(-1))\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot [2{z}^{2}]_{z=-1}^{z=2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1\\cdot (8-2) \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430949 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186430945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430949 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162573" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430949 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430949 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/6d/6dc4f6d9e8520dead79789efd4f08c07ea7921d28f4cf6f8da06de533d012e90.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨y-z,\\,y-2x,\\,x+4z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Given that $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ through $S.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430949 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "28a83ce5-6b28-57bd-96e8-b831ae6041a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430949 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-2x)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x+4z) \\\\  & =0+1+4 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the rectangular solid enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}5\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =5{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 4\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430950 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186430941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430950 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162605" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430950 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430950 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $y=1,$ $y=3,$ $z=0,$ $z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(y+{z}^{2})\\,i-x\\,j+3{x}^{2}{e}^{z}\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430950 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2ce23f40-f256-54cd-9b13-888ae9d3dff9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430950 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y+{z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-x)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3{x}^{2}{e}^{z}) \\\\  & =0+0+3{x}^{2}{e}^{z} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}{e}^{z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}3{x}^{2}{e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}3{x}^{2}{e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{3}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}]_{x=0}^{x=1}\\cdot (3-1)\\cdot [{e}^{z}]_{z=0}^{z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =({1}^{3}-{0}^{3})\\cdot 2\\cdot ({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2\\cdot (3-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430951 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186430944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430951 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162574" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430951 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430951 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the positively (outward) oriented surface $S$ given by ${x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}+{z}^{2}=9$ and the vector field $F=(yz-2x)\\,i+3y\\,j+(x+z)\\,k.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430951 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5f2b40e8-b423-5c7a-b959-43a67763f792" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430951 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(yz-2x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x+z) \\\\  & =-2+3+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $3$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}2\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =2{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {3}^{3} \\\\  & =72\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430952 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186430942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430952 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162575" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430952 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430952 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=2,$ $z=1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F={x}^{2}\\,i+xyz\\,j+2y\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\bbox[2pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430952 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cd56ae3f-1187-5181-af79-1866b5188a10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430952 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xyz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2y) \\\\  & =2x+xz+0 \\\\  & =2x+xz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}(2x+xz)\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,2x+xz\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
